{
  "slug": "artist:vinicius-de-moraes",
  "name": "Vinicius de Moraes",
  "editorial_article": "Born in Rio de Janeiro in 1913, the diplomat-poet Vinicius de Moraes became an unlikely architect of the bossa nova movement by applying his literary sensibilities to samba rhythms. Following his initial collaboration with Antonio Carlos Jobim on the 1956 musical 'Orfeu da Conceição', his lyrics for the seminal Elizete Cardoso album 'Canção do Amor Demais' helped formalize the genre's shift toward intimate, syncopated songcraft. Moraes later toured Europe with Chico Buarque and Nara Leão, performing his trademark 'pocket-shows' while seated at a table with a bottle of whiskey. His enduring partnership with the guitarist Toquinho yielded numerous popular albums throughout the 1970s, including their collaborative release 'La voglia, la pazzia, l'incoscienza, l'allegria' in 1976.",
